.nh
.TH "PB-ACCESS-MACHINE" "1" "Sep 2026" "Paperboat" "Paperboat CLI"

.SH NAME
pb-access-machine - Forward a local port to an authorized machine service


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpb access machine <machine> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Forward a loopback address on this computer to one TCP port of an enrolled machine, for
example a database that only listens on that machine. The command runs in the foreground, prints
the address it listens on, and stops on Ctrl-C (exit status 130). Only this computer can reach
the listener, up to 128 connections at once.

.PP
--port is required. --listen must be a literal loopback address, 127.0.0.1 or ::1 with an
optional port; the default 127.0.0.1:0 picks a free port. The target machine must offer the
service and its connection must be authorized. For a team machine that includes a grant of
private_access.

.PP
JSON output is supported with --json.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for machine

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	print canonical JSON

.PP
\fB--listen\fP="127.0.0.1:0"
	loopback address to listen on, 127.0.0.1 or ::1 with optional port (default: a free port)

.PP
\fB--port\fP=0
	TCP port on the machine (required, 1-65535)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	path to the CLI config file

.PP
\fB--no-customization\fP[=false]
	ignore local shortcuts, command defaults, and TUI preferences

.PP
\fB--server\fP=""
	paperboat-server base URL override

.PP
\fB--workspace\fP=""
	resource workspace selector: personal or a team slug


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  pb access machine db --port 5432
  pb access machine db --port 6379 --listen 127.0.0.1:6379
  pb access machine db --port 5432 --json
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpb-access(1)\fP
